TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 32 ----
    '32:1': "The debate ends not with an answer but with three men giving up. They stop because Job will not yield.",
    '32:2': "A fourth speaker nobody has mentioned is suddenly named, with a pedigree, and what is named first is his anger.",
    '32:3': "He is angry at both sides: the friends condemned Job while finding nothing to say.",
    '32:4': "He held his tongue because they were older. The deference was real before the outburst.",
    '32:5': "It is their silence, not Job's argument, that finally sets him off.",
    '32:6': "\"I am young, and ye are very old.\" He opens by conceding the one thing that should disqualify him.",
    '32:7': "\"Days should speak\" — he states the rule he is about to break before breaking it.",
    '32:8': "His warrant is not his years but a spirit in man. Elihu claims his understanding is given, not accumulated.",
    '32:9': "Age and greatness do not guarantee judgement. Elihu makes room for himself by unseating a whole convention.",
    '32:10': "He asks only to be allowed an opinion. The claim is modest and the speech runs six chapters.",
    '32:11': "He listened to the whole debate. Whatever else he is, Elihu is the one man who has heard everybody out.",
    '32:12': "His charge is precise: nobody answered Job's words. They convicted him without meeting a single argument.",
    '32:13': "He warns them off congratulating themselves on wisdom. Only God, not men, can thrust Job down.",
    '32:14': "He will not recycle their speeches. Elihu insists his case is new, and asks to be judged on that.",
    '32:15': "The three are amazed and have nothing left. The narrator's voice cuts in for a moment inside the speech.",
    '32:16': "He waited while they stood still. The pause before Elihu speaks is given two whole verses.",
    '32:17': "\"I also will shew mine opinion.\" He repeats himself, and a man this eager to begin is worth watching.",
    '32:18': "He is full of words and says the spirit inside him leaves him no choice.",
    '32:19': "New wine with no vent. His own picture of himself is of pressure that must escape.",
    '32:20': "\"I will speak, that I may be refreshed.\" He says outright that the speech is partly for his own relief.",
    '32:21': "He promises to use no flattering titles, which is a promise to spare nobody in the circle.",
    '32:22': "He fears that flattery would get him killed by his Maker. The scruple is genuine whatever comes of it.",
    # ---- Chapter 33 ----
    '33:1': "Elihu calls Job by name. In thirty chapters of debate the three friends never once did.",
    '33:2': "A whole verse to announce that he has begun speaking. Elihu clears a great deal of throat.",
    '33:3': "He stakes his case on plain speech and an upright heart rather than on standing.",
    '33:4': "Made by the same breath, he claims no advantage of nature over the man he is answering.",
    '33:5': "He invites a reply. Elihu is the only speaker who asks Job to argue back.",
    '33:6': "Job asked for someone to stand between him and God. Elihu offers himself — clay, like Job, and not frightening.",
    '33:7': "Job's fear was of being overwhelmed by power before he could speak. Elihu promises not to do that to him.",
    '33:8': "He quotes Job before answering him, which is more than the other three managed.",
    '33:9': "The quotation is sharpened. Job claimed innocence of the charges, not that no iniquity was in him.",
    '33:10': "\"He counteth me for his enemy\" — Elihu has Job's complaint exactly right, whatever he does with it.",
    '33:11': "Feet in the stocks: Job's own image, repeated back as the grievance to be answered.",
    '33:12': "\"God is greater than man.\" Elihu's answer begins where Bildad's ended, but he will not stop there.",
    '33:13': "Job's error, Elihu says, is demanding an accounting. God is not obliged to explain his matters.",
    '33:14': "The turn in the book's argument: God does speak, more than once, and the man fails to notice.",
    '33:15': "Dreams and night visions are named as one of God's ways of speaking to an ordinary man.",
    '33:16': "\"Sealeth their instruction\" — the dream is not information but a warning stamped and delivered.",
    '33:17': "The aim of the warning is to stop a man in his tracks and take his pride off him.",
    '33:18': "Suffering is described as prevention, keeping a soul back from the pit rather than paying it out.",
    '33:19': "Pain on the bed is God's second language. Elihu makes affliction a message rather than a verdict.",
    '33:20': "The sick man cannot face food. Elihu describes the illness in detail before he interprets it.",
    '33:21': "Flesh gone and bones showing. Elihu has evidently looked at Job while the others talked.",
    '33:22': "The man reaches the edge of the grave, and this is where Elihu's account turns.",
    '33:23': "\"One among a thousand\" — a messenger, an interpreter. What the man needs at the edge is somebody to speak for him.",
    '33:24': "\"I have found a ransom.\" Deliverance comes because a price has been located, not because the man improved.",
    '33:25': "Flesh fresher than a child's. Elihu's God restores rather than merely spares.",
    '33:26': "The restored man sees God's face with joy, and righteousness is rendered to him rather than earned.",
    '33:27': "The confession Elihu puts in the man's mouth admits both the sin and that it never paid.",
    '33:28': "The outcome is not merely survival but light. The pit is escaped, not endured.",
    '33:29': "\"Oftentimes\" — Elihu insists this is God's ordinary practice, not a rare intervention.",
    '33:30': "Twice from the pit, into the light of the living. His whole case is that God is working to bring a man back.",
    '33:31': "\"Hold thy peace, and I will speak.\" He asked for a reply in verse 5 and now asks for silence.",
    '33:32': "\"I desire to justify thee.\" Whatever the three came to do, Elihu says he came wanting Job acquitted.",
    '33:33': "The offer ends as a lesson: be quiet and be taught. Wisdom is on his side of the conversation.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 34 ----
    '34:2': "He turns from Job to the bystanders and argues the case before an audience instead of the man.",
    '34:3': "\"The ear trieth words, as the mouth tasteth meat.\" He asks to be tested, which few speakers here do.",
    '34:4': "He proposes that they settle it together, as a jury weighing evidence rather than as a man pronouncing.",
    '34:5': "The quotation is fair: Job did say God had taken away his judgement.",
    '34:6': "\"My wound is incurable without transgression\" — the grievance Elihu must answer is that the punishment has no crime.",
    '34:7': "\"Drinketh up scorning like water.\" The promise of no flattering titles is being kept in full.",
    '34:8': "The charge is company, not conduct: Job's words put him on the side of men he has never walked with.",
    '34:9': "Elihu has Job saying that delighting in God profits nothing — which is exactly the accuser's thesis in chapter 1.",
    '34:10': "The axiom Elihu will not surrender: God cannot do wrong. Everything else must be arranged around it.",
    '34:11': "Retribution restated as flat arithmetic. Here Elihu sounds like the three men he was angry with.",
    '34:12': "Justice is argued from God's nature rather than from the evidence Job has laid out.",
    '34:13': "Nobody appointed God over the earth, so there is no higher office to appeal his conduct to.",
    '34:14': "God has only to draw back his breath and stop. Sustaining is presented as an ongoing decision.",
    '34:15': "If he gathered his Spirit home, all flesh would end together. Existence itself is on loan.",
    '34:16': "\"If now thou hast understanding\" — the invitation and the insult arrive in the same breath.",
    '34:17': "Government presupposes justice, Elihu argues: one who hated right could not hold the world together.",
    '34:18': "You do not call a king wicked to his face. The argument runs upward from ordinary courtesy.",
    '34:19': "God takes no bribes and knows no ranks, because rich and poor alike are the work of his hands.",
    '34:20': "The mighty are removed without a hand being laid on them, at midnight, with no process anyone can see.",
    '34:21': "God's eyes are on every step. Elihu makes omniscience the guarantee that judgement is accurate.",
    '34:22': "There is no darkness deep enough to work in. What Job feels as surveillance, Elihu offers as fairness.",
    '34:23': "God needs no fixed court date, Elihu says — which is precisely the hearing Job has been asking for.",
    '34:24': "He breaks the mighty without inquiry and puts others in their place. Power changes hands without explanation.",
    '34:25': "He overturns them in the night. The judgement Job cannot see is said to be happening in the dark.",
    '34:26': "\"In the open sight of others\" — Elihu needs the punishment to be public or the doctrine cannot be checked.",
    '34:27': "The root offence is turning back and refusing to consider God's ways. Inattention, not atrocity.",
    '34:28': "\"He heareth the cry of the afflicted\" — flatly contradicting Job's charge in chapter 24 that God does not.",
    '34:29': "When God is quiet, no one can make trouble, and when he hides, no one can compel him to appear.",
    '34:30': "Even God's hiddenness is said to serve a purpose: keeping the godless man off the throne.",
    '34:31': "\"I have borne chastisement, I will not offend any more\" — the speech Elihu is waiting for Job to make.",
    '34:32': "\"That which I see not teach thou me.\" A prayer about unknown sin, which Job has refused to pray.",
    '34:33': "God will not settle accounts to Job's specification. Elihu is right, and it is not yet an answer.",
    '34:34': "He appeals again to the sensible men listening, as though a show of hands could close the matter.",
    '34:35': "\"Job hath spoken without knowledge.\" The voice from the whirlwind will use nearly the same words.",
    '34:36': "\"Tried unto the end\" — he asks that the suffering continue until Job breaks. This is the book's coldest sentence.",
    '34:37': "Clapping and multiplied words: the charge has moved from Job's theology to his manner.",
    # ---- Chapter 35 ----
    '35:2': "Job said his cause was just. Elihu has him claiming to be more righteous than God, which he never said.",
    '35:3': "The quoted question is the accuser's question from chapter 1 turned inside out: what is godliness worth?",
    '35:4': "He answers Job and the three together, having decided that all four are making the same mistake.",
    '35:5': "\"Behold the clouds which are higher than thou.\" The argument from height is about to become an argument from distance.",
    '35:6': "Your sin does not damage God. Elihu removes the personal injury Job has been assuming all along.",
    '35:7': "And your righteousness adds nothing to him. Nobody puts God in his debt by behaving well.",
    '35:8': "Sin and virtue land horizontally, on the man next to you. That is where the damage and the good actually go.",
    '35:9': "The oppressed cry out under the arm of the mighty. Elihu grants the suffering before he analyses the cry.",
    '35:10': "\"Who giveth songs in the night\" — the cry is a complaint about pain, not a search for God, and Elihu hears the difference.",
    '35:11': "Men are taught more than the beasts, and the capacity is given rather than developed.",
    '35:12': "They cry and get no answer, and the reason offered is the pride still in the crying.",
    '35:13': "An empty cry gets an empty answer. Elihu's God is not obliged by noise.",
    '35:14': "\"Therefore trust thou in him.\" The case is filed even when the judge cannot be seen; Job is told to wait.",
    '35:15': "Because the reckoning has not come, Elihu says, Job assumes there will be none.",
    '35:16': "The charge is not that Job lied but that he talked — many words, and no knowledge under them.",
    # ---- Chapter 36 ----
    '36:2': "\"On God's behalf.\" Elihu takes up a brief nobody assigned him, and the book lets him deliver it.",
    '36:3': "He will fetch his knowledge from far off and ascribe righteousness to his Maker — the conclusion is fixed in advance.",
    '36:4': "\"He that is perfect in knowledge is with thee\" — said of himself, in a phrase he will shortly use of God.",
    '36:5': "\"God is mighty, and despiseth not any.\" Power that does not look down on the small is the best thing Elihu says.",
    '36:6': "The same God who ends the wicked gives the poor their rights. Strength is aimed in a direction.",
    '36:7': "He does not take his eyes off the righteous, and can seat them with kings for good.",
    '36:8': "\"And if they be bound in fetters\" — the righteous suffer too. Elihu grants what the three would not.",
    '36:9': "Affliction is God showing a man what he has done, which assumes there is always something to show.",
    '36:10': "The ear is opened by discipline. Elihu's whole case is that pain is instruction rather than sentence.",
    '36:11': "Obedience is promised prosperity, and the old equation quietly reassembles itself.",
    '36:12': "To die without knowledge is the loss named — not the sword but never having understood.",
    '36:13': "\"They cry not when he bindeth them.\" The fatal thing is not the chain but the silence in it.",
    '36:14': "Their lives end young and among the temple prostitutes. Elihu's picture of the wasted man is concrete.",
    '36:15': "God opens ears in oppression. The affliction is the instrument, not the obstacle.",
    '36:16': "A broad place and a full table were on offer, Elihu says. He knows what God intended for Job.",
    '36:17': "The turn against Job is sudden: he is now said to have earned the wicked man's sentence.",
    '36:18': "He warns that no ransom will be large enough — having promised one in chapter 33.",
    '36:19': "Riches and force buy nothing here. Whatever Elihu gets wrong, he is not flattering a rich man.",
    '36:20': "Job longed for the night and for death. Elihu tells him not to want what he has been praying for.",
    '36:21': "The accusation lands: Job is said to have chosen rebellion in preference to suffering quietly.",
    '36:22': "\"Who teacheth like him?\" Elihu's God is above all a teacher, and this is his final theme.",
    '36:23': "Nobody assigned God his method, and nobody has standing to charge him with wrong.",
    '36:24': "Praise is put as a command: remember to magnify the work. Elihu prescribes what he wants Job to feel.",
    '36:25': "Every man sees God's work, and every man sees it from far off. The viewing is real and the distance is permanent.",
    '36:26': "\"God is great, and we know him not.\" Elihu concedes the ignorance he has spent five chapters not showing.",
    '36:27': "Rain is traced back to single drops drawn up. The wonder is in the mechanism, not the downpour.",
    '36:28': "The clouds distil on men abundantly. What falls on everybody is given as evidence of care.",
    '36:29': "Nobody understands how a cloud spreads. Elihu's questions begin to sound like the ones that follow.",
    '36:30': "Lightning spread out to the roots of the sea — light reaching where no man has been able to look.",
    '36:31': "The same weather judges nations and feeds them. Elihu will not let the two be separated.",
    '36:32': "He holds the lightning in his hands and tells it where to strike. Nothing in the storm is loose.",
    '36:33': "Even the cattle know the storm is coming. The warning is issued to creatures that cannot reason.",
    # ---- Chapter 37 ----
    '37:1': "\"My heart trembleth.\" The man explaining the storm admits he is frightened of it.",
    '37:2': "Thunder is treated as speech. Elihu is closer than he knows to what is about to happen.",
    '37:3': "The lightning is aimed to the ends of the earth. What looks random is described as directed.",
    '37:4': "\"He will not stay them when his voice is heard.\" God does not hold back the thunder to be polite.",
    '37:5': "Great things doeth he, which we cannot comprehend — the sentence Elihu keeps returning to.",
    '37:6': "\"He saith to the snow, Be thou on the earth.\" Weather arrives as a command, not a process.",
    '37:7': "Winter seals up the hand of every man. Work is stopped so that men are made to look at God's work instead.",
    '37:8': "The beasts go into their dens. The storm empties the world of everything that can take shelter.",
    '37:9': "The whirlwind comes out of the south — and in the next chapter one arrives.",
    '37:10': "Frost is made by the breath of God, and the wide water narrows into ice at a word.",
    '37:11': "The heavy cloud is worn out with carrying water. The sky is described as labouring.",
    '37:12': "The clouds turn about by his counsels. Nothing in the weather is idle or accidental.",
    '37:13': "Correction, or his land, or mercy. The same rain arrives for three different reasons, and you cannot tell which.",
    '37:14': "\"Stand still, and consider.\" The instruction is to stop arguing and look, which is nearly what God will ask.",
    '37:15': "Elihu begins asking Job what he knows. The questions are the right shape and the wrong voice.",
    '37:16': "\"The balancings of the clouds\" — a man who cannot weigh a cloud is being asked to audit providence.",
    '37:17': "He points at the warmth of Job's own clothes. You cannot account for your own comfort, let alone God's dealings.",
    '37:18': "The sky is hammered out like a bronze mirror, and Job was not there holding the other end.",
    '37:19': "\"We cannot order our speech by reason of darkness.\" Elihu admits at last that he does not know what to say.",
    '37:20': "A man who insisted on speaking now says a man who speaks would be swallowed up.",
    '37:21': "The light behind the cloud is unlookable at once the wind clears it. He is describing what is about to arrive.",
    '37:22': "Golden splendour out of the north, and terrible majesty with God. Elihu's last picture is of approach.",
    '37:23': "His final claim is that God cannot be found out and will not afflict. The whirlwind interrupts him.",
    '37:24': "Elihu ends with God caring nothing for the self-declared wise, which is an odd note for this speaker to end on.",
})
